The tool applies your one answer to L4. Every other line stays byte-identical. Changing one is rejected\n  outright even if the change is an improvement.\n\nWHAT THE GRADER ENFORCES ON YOUR ANSWER\n  1. ONE line back, and it replaces L4. The draft stays 6 lines \u2014 a different\n     line count is rejected outright: this loop revises lines, it does not restructure\n     the draft.\n  2. ONLY L4 changes (declared returns are mirrored). Any other line that comes back different is rejected\n     outright, whatever else the revision achieved.\n  3. The end word L4 MOVES TO is not one of the FORBIDDEN words above. That is\n     checked before anything else about the line and rejects on its own. KEEPING the\n     word already there is not a move and is not rejected here \u2014 see item 4.\n  4. The line must FIX at least one finding. A rewrite that is differently defective\n     is rejected as 'nothing was fixed'.\n  5. It may not trade one defect for another: new FLAG findings anywhere in the draft\n     \u2014 including on lines you did not touch \u2014 reject it once they exceed the declared\n     `allow_net_new` (0 by default). New NOTES do not reject.\n\nHOW TO ANSWER\n  Send the line and nothing else: one line, no quotation marks around it, no line\n  number, no commentary, no code fence. If you must explain yourself first, put the\n  line LAST, alone on its own line, beginning with `LINE: `. Anything ambiguous \u2014\n  two candidate lines, a quoted line, an explanation with no marker \u2014 is discarded\n  unread and costs you this attempt.\n  The line is at most 200 characters; a longer one is not accepted.",
